 ## Overview
-The Diffraction-Specific Approach (also known as the Hogel Vector Approach) is a method for generating Computer-Generated Holograms (CGH) that bypasses the direct simulation of optical interference. Instead of summing complex wavefronts from object elements against a reference wavefront for every pixel, it treats the holographic fringe as being subsampled spatially into functional holographic elements called "hogels" and spectrally into an array of "hogel vectors" [S1]. This approach builds on [Holographic Computer-Generated Holography (CGH) Calculation](6884-holographic-computer-generated-holography-cgh-calculation.md) by leveraging standard 3D computer graphics rendering (orthographic projections) to generate the data, significantly increasing computation speed and achieving bandwidth compression compared to the Interference-Based Approach [S1]. It trades off image sharpness for speed, introducing a blur that can be matched to the acuity of the human visual system (HVS) [S1].
+The Diffraction-Specific Approach is a method for generating Computer-Generated Holography (CGH) fringes that avoids the direct, computationally expensive simulation of optical interference between object elements and a reference wave. Instead, it works backwards from the 3D image, treating the hologram fringe as being subsampled spatially into functional holographic elements called "hogels" and spectrally into an array of "hogel vectors" [S1]. It sets itself apart from the Interference-Based Approach by replacing billions of complex arithmetic operations (trigonometric functions and square roots) with standard 3D computer graphics rendering (orthographic projections) and efficient decoding via texture mapping [S1]. It trades image sharpness for significant bandwidth compression and computation speed, with added blur matched to the acuity of the human visual system (HVS) [S1].
 
